    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Title">Performance</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Subtitle">Measured frame rates, dropped frames and latency for every capture, plus resource use, Auto Performance and what this PC can handle. Everything here is measured, except the tier, which is marked as an estimate.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Resources">What VIZEN and your PC use right now</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.VizenCpu">Processor used by VIZEN</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.SystemCpu">Processor used in total</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Gpu">Graphics card, in total</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Memory">Memory (RAM) used by VIZEN</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.ResourcesNote">These values are measured once per second, using the same Windows counters as Task Manager. The levels are a guide, based on these limits: VIZEN processor use is low under 5% and medium under 15%; total processor use is low under 60% and medium under 85%; graphics card use is low under 70% and medium under 90%; VIZEN memory is low under 5% of RAM and medium under 15%. Anything above these limits is high.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.VizenCpuHelp">How much of the processor's capacity VIZEN uses. Under 5% is usually not noticeable, even while gaming.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.SystemCpuHelp">All running programs combined, including your game. Above 85%, the PC may start to feel slow.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.GpuHelp">How busy the graphics card is. The game usually uses the most; VIZEN's share is shown below. Above 90%, the game may drop frames.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.MemoryHelp">How much of the PC's memory VIZEN uses while it is running.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.MemoryShare">{0}% of this PC's {1} GB</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Level.Low">Low</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Level.Medium">Medium</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Level.High">High</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Captures">Active captures</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.NoCaptures">Nothing is being captured. Start an overlay or an OBS output, or open a preview in Sources.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Col.Source">Source fps</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Col.Shown">Shown fps</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Col.Dropped">Dropped</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Col.Latency">VIZEN latency</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Col.Jitter">Frame-pacing jitter</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Col.Work">Work per frame</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.CapturesNote">All overlays, outputs and previews of the same source share a single capture. Windows sends a new frame only when the source changes, so a static window shows a low frame rate; this is normal and not a VIZEN problem. The frame rate shown can never exceed the source's, because VIZEN never invents or repeats frames to raise the number.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.Auto">Auto Performance</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.AutoEnabled">Auto Performance</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.AutoEnabledDescription">Monitors every capture. If VIZEN itself drops frames for 3 seconds, it identifies the likely cause (busy GPU, busy CPU, or copying or display that is too slow) and lets you know. Captures limited by their source are left unchanged.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.AllowReduce">Allow lowering to 30 fps</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.AllowReduceDescription">Off by default. When turned on, Auto Performance may limit a struggling capture to 30 fps (resolution and quality stay the same), tries the full frame rate again after a minute, and notifies you each time. Turning it off removes all limits immediately.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.AutoLog">What Auto Performance did</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.RemoveCaps">Full frame rate now</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Perf.AutoLogEmpty">Nothing yet: no capture has had problems in this session.</sys:String>
